<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">combatpace</ansi>

A combat round is decided in an instant, but you don't read it all at
once. It plays out to you one action at a time, each fighter's turn on
its own beat, so the fight reads like one: a blow, its answer, the next
fighter's move. How long a beat is, is up to you:

  <ansi fg="command">set combatpace fast</ansi>     a turn every 0.6 seconds
  <ansi fg="command">set combatpace normal</ansi>   a turn every 1.5 seconds
  <ansi fg="command">set combatpace slow</ansi>     a turn every 2.25 seconds
  <ansi fg="command">set combatpace off</ansi>      the whole round at once

Normal is the default. If you play with a screen reader (<ansi fg="command">set</ansi> shows
ScreenReader), the default is off. <ansi fg="command">set</ansi> shows your pace.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">How it plays out</ansi>

- Each fighter's turn is a beat: its line comes, and the lines it causes
  (the damage under a spell, a stagger, a fall) follow a quarter second
  apart, with a pause before a pain reaction or a death line so the blow
  lands first.
- The next round begins only when the last one has played out, plus a
  short breath, and never sooner than 3 seconds after the last began. A
  round is never squeezed: ten fighters take ten beats. So the fight is
  as long as its actions, and no faster than you can read them.
- Everyone in a fight shares its rounds. Each of you reads them at your
  own pace, and the round waits for the slowest reader among you; off
  reads at once and waits like the rest, so the fight stays followable.
- A companion's death notice, the closing line, and the battle summary
  come in their place, after the lines that led to them.
- Your prompt, and the web client's health bars and battle view, show
  how things stood when the round began until its lines have all come
  in. So they never tell you how a blow landed before you read it.
- If you leave the room, log out, or change your pace, anything still
  waiting comes at once. Nothing is lost.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What is never held back</ansi>

What people say, tells, and anything your own commands show you come at
once, even mid-round. You can type at any time. Everything else that
happens meanwhile (a foe choosing its next target, a notice that you are
bleeding, news of a death) waits behind the round's lines, so nothing
arrives ahead of what led to it.

Pacing only changes when you read the round and how soon the next comes.
The fight itself is decided the moment the round starts, the same for
everyone, and who acts first in it is a matter of tempo (<ansi fg="command">help tempo</ansi>).
